<sect1 id="asustor">
<title>The ASUSTOR NAS LCD driver</title>

<para>
The <code>asustor</code> driver supports the 16x2 front-panel LCD module
found in ASUSTOR NAS systems.  The display is controlled by an embedded
microcontroller exposed through a serial port, usually
<filename>/dev/ttyS1</filename>, using a packet-based protocol rather than
plain text writes.
</para>

<para>
The protocol implementation is based on behavior documented by the
<code>openlcmd</code> implementation from
<ulink url="https://github.com/mafredri/lcm">github.com/mafredri/lcm</ulink>.
Each command is acknowledged by the display controller.  Button events share
the same serial stream as command acknowledgements, so the driver routes all
incoming packets through a common receive path.
</para>

<sect2 id="asustor-features">
<title>Features</title>

<itemizedlist>
<listitem><para>16x2 character display output.</para></listitem>
<listitem><para>Display wake and initialization during LCDd startup.</para></listitem>
<listitem><para>Front-panel navigation buttons mapped to LCDproc key names.</para></listitem>
<listitem><para>Serial communication at the ASUSTOR controller's fixed 115200 baud rate.</para></listitem>
</itemizedlist>

<para>
LCDd backlight-off requests are ignored by default because ASUSTOR uses the
same command to blank the whole front-panel LCD.  This keeps the display
readable during normal LCDproc screen rotation.
</para>
</sect2>

<sect2 id="asustor-requirements">
<title>Requirements</title>

<para>
The ASUSTOR LCD controller must be exposed as a serial TTY.  On tested
systems this is <filename>/dev/ttyS1</filename>.  No USB HID, libusb, or
kernel LCD driver is required for the display protocol.
</para>
</sect2>

<sect2 id="asustor-buttons">
<title>Buttons</title>

<para>
The ASUSTOR controller reports four front-panel buttons.  The driver maps
them to LCDproc key names as follows:
</para>

<informaltable>
<tgroup cols="2">
<thead>
<row><entry>ASUSTOR button</entry><entry>LCDproc key</entry></row>
</thead>
<tbody>
<row><entry>Up</entry><entry><literal>Up</literal></entry></row>
<row><entry>Down</entry><entry><literal>Down</literal></entry></row>
<row><entry>Back</entry><entry><literal>Escape</literal></entry></row>
<row><entry>Enter</entry><entry><literal>Enter</literal></entry></row>
</tbody>
</tgroup>
</informaltable>
</sect2>

<sect2 id="asustor-config">
<title>Configuration in LCDd.conf</title>

<programlisting>
[server]
Driver=asustor
DriverPath=/lib/lcdproc/
WaitTime=10
TitleSpeed=4
Heartbeat=off
Backlight=open
ToggleRotateKey=Menu
PrevScreenKey=Escape
NextScreenKey=Enter
ScrollUpKey=Up
ScrollDownKey=Down

[asustor]
Device=/dev/ttyS1
Speed=115200
Size=16x2
</programlisting>

<para>
The ASUSTOR front panel is only 16 columns wide.  A longer
<property>WaitTime</property> keeps each lcdproc screen visible long enough
to read, and a lower <property>TitleSpeed</property> slows horizontal title
scrolling.  Values around <literal>WaitTime=10</literal> and
<literal>TitleSpeed=4</literal> are a practical starting point for the stock
16x2 display.
</para>

<para>
The example maps the front-panel Back and Enter buttons to previous and next
screen navigation.  It deliberately does not assign a
<property>MenuKey</property>, because LCDproc reserves that key exclusively
for the built-in menu and would otherwise prevent Back from acting as previous
screen navigation.
</para>

<sect3 id="asustor-config-section">
<title>[asustor]</title>

<variablelist>
<varlistentry>
  <term>
    <property>Device</property> =
    <parameter><replaceable>DEVICE</replaceable></parameter>
  </term>
  <listitem><para>
    Serial port connected to the ASUSTOR LCD controller.  Default:
    <filename>/dev/ttyS1</filename>.
  </para></listitem>
</varlistentry>

<varlistentry>
  <term>
    <property>Speed</property> =
    <parameter><replaceable>NUMBER</replaceable></parameter>
  </term>
  <listitem><para>
    Documented for clarity.  The ASUSTOR controller protocol uses
    <literal>115200</literal> baud.
  </para></listitem>
</varlistentry>

<varlistentry>
  <term>
    <property>Size</property> = &parameters.size;
  </term>
  <listitem><para>
    Documented for clarity.  The supported ASUSTOR front panel is treated as
    a fixed <literal>16x2</literal> display.
  </para></listitem>
</varlistentry>
</variablelist>
</sect3>

</sect2>

<sect2 id="asustor-testing">
<title>Testing</title>

<para>
A new ASUSTOR installation should be tested with LCDd in the foreground before
enabling it at boot.  The driver should initialize the panel, keep the display
readable, rotate normal lcdproc screens, and report front-panel button events.
</para>

<programlisting>
LCDd -f -r 4 -c /path/to/LCDd.conf
lcdproc T U M
</programlisting>

<para>
Confirm the following behavior:
</para>

<itemizedlist>
<listitem><para>The display wakes and shows LCDproc output instead of the vendor boot message.</para></listitem>
<listitem><para>Both 16-character rows update without visible packet corruption.</para></listitem>
<listitem><para>Back and Enter can move between screens when mapped as previous and next screen keys.</para></listitem>
<listitem><para>Up and Down can scroll LCDproc menu entries when the LCDproc menu is active.</para></listitem>
<listitem><para>Restarting LCDd recovers cleanly if stale bytes remain on the serial line.</para></listitem>
</itemizedlist>

</sect2>

</sect1>
